Referring to the diagram in the problem, after the emission of a single photon $h \nu^{\prime}$, the energy and momentum conservation formulas are:

$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\frac{1}{2} mv_{1}^{2} + E = \frac{1}{2} mv_{1}^{\prime 2} + h \nu^{\prime} \\
mv_{1} = mv_{1}^{\prime} \cos \varphi + \frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{c} \cos \theta \\
0 = mv_{1}^{\prime} \sin \varphi - \frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{c} \sin \theta
\end{array}
\right.
$$

Since the changes $v_{1}^{\prime} = v_{1} + \Delta v^{\prime}$ and $\nu^{\prime} = \nu + \Delta \nu^{\prime}$ are very small, when $\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}$, the deflection angle $\varphi$ reaches its maximum, which is:

$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
mv_{1} = mv_{1}^{\prime} \cos \varphi \\
