Let the velocity of the space station during circular motion be $v$. We have

$$
\frac{m v^{2}}{R} = \frac{G m M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R^{2}}, \quad v = \sqrt{\frac{G M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R}}
$$

Let the velocity of the object launched forward by the space station be $v_{1}$, and the velocity of the space station after launching the object be $v_{2}$. According to the conservation of momentum,

\begin{equation*}
m v = m_{1} v_{1} + \left(m - m_{1}\right) v_{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

After the launch, the mechanical energy of the space station is conserved, and the angular momentum is conserved:

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\left(m-m_{1}\right) v_{2}^{2} - \frac{G\left(m-m_{1}\right) M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R} & = \frac{1}{2}\left(m-m_{1}\right) v_{3}^{2} - \frac{G\left(m-m_{1}\right) M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R_{\mathrm{m}}} \tag{2}\\
\left(m-m_{1}\right) v_{2} R & = \left(m-m_{1}\right) v_{3} R_{\mathrm{m}} \tag{3}
\end{align*}

where $v_{3}$ is the velocity of the space station upon landing on the moon. From equations (2) and (3), we can solve for

\begin{equation*}
v_{2} = \sqrt{\frac{2 G M_{\mathrm{m}} R_{\mathrm{m}}}{R\left(R + R_{\mathrm{m}}\right)}} \tag{4}
\end{equation*}

By solving equation (1) for $v_{1}$ and substituting it into equation (4),

$$
\begin{gathered}
v_{1} = \frac{m v}{m_{1}} - \frac{m - m_{1}}{m_{1}} v_{2} \\
= \frac{m}{m_{1}} \sqrt{\frac{G M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R}} - \frac{m - m_{1}}{m_{1}} \sqrt{\frac{2 G M_{\mathrm{m}} R_{\mathrm{m}}}{R\left(R + R_{\mathrm{m}}\right)}}
\end{gathered}
$$

